@* Exercise 035 — ErrorBoundaryLeakage (reference solution).
   Goal:   Wrap ChildContent in an ErrorBoundary whose ErrorContent never
           leaks what actually went wrong. OnInitialized must set
           OperatorMessage and CorrelationId up front - neither one may ever
           be derived from the exception ErrorContent eventually receives,
           because by then it is too late to un-leak it.
   Drills: ErrorBoundary, suppressing exception detail in the render tree.
   Passes: attack facts - the rendered error content contains neither the
                          exception's message, nor its type name, nor a
                          stack frame;
           use facts     - the error content shows a stable, non-empty
                          operator-facing message and a correlation id; when
                          the child does not throw, its normal content
                          renders and no error content appears at all. *@

<ErrorBoundary>
    <ChildContent>
        <div id="normal-content">@ChildContent</div>
    </ChildContent>
    <ErrorContent>
        <div id="error-content">
            <span id="error-message">@OperatorMessage</span>
            <span id="error-correlation-id">@CorrelationId</span>
        </div>
    </ErrorContent>
</ErrorBoundary>

@code {
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? ChildContent { get; set; }

    private string OperatorMessage = "";
    private string CorrelationId = "";

    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        // Fixed and generic - never built from the exception ErrorContent's
        // Context parameter receives. That parameter is intentionally never
        // read anywhere in this file.
        OperatorMessage =
            "Something went wrong while loading this page. Please try again, and contact support if it keeps happening.";
        CorrelationId = Guid.NewGuid().ToString("N");
    }
}
